{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>That has a cleft hoof.</b> As its Targum renders: סְדִיקָא." "<b>Hoof.</b> פלנט\"א בְּלַעַ\"ז." "<b>And wholly split.</b> That is separated from above and from below in two claws — as its Targum renders: וּמַטִּלְפָן טִלְפִין — for there are those whose hooves are cleft from above but not split and completely separated, being joined from below." "<b>Bringing up the cud.</b> Raises and regurgitates the food from its innards and returns it into its mouth to crush it and grind it fine." "<b>Cud.</b> That is its name; and it may be from the derivation of ״מֵי הַנִּגָּרִים״ (II Samuel 14), for it is dragged along after the mouth; and its Targum is פִּשְׁרָא — for by means of the gerah the food is loosened and dissolved." "<b>Among the animal.</b> It is an extra word for derashah — to permit the fetus found in its mother's womb (Sifra; Ḥullin 69)." "<b>You may eat.</b> And not an unclean animal — but is this not a warning? Rather, it is to transgress on it both a positive and a negative commandment (Sifra; Zevaḥim 34)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {1 "the la'az is Old French plante, 'sole of the foot / hoof'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:05:49.810838982Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}